course: dictionary
lesson: 15513
title: Subscribe: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them show you which meaning fits. «Subscribe» is a good example: it can mean paying for something, signing up for updates, or agreeing with an idea.
explain: «Mum will subscribe to the magazine so it arrives every month.» Here subscribe means to pay regularly, because the magazine comes each month and costs money. «Please subscribe to my channel for more videos.» Here it means to sign up to get updates, often for free.
explain: «I don't subscribe to that view» is different again. It is a formal phrase meaning you do not agree with or support that idea. Ask yourself: is someone paying, signing up, or believing? The clues tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Subscribe is a verb: subscribe, subscribes, subscribed, subscribing.
fact: «Subscribe» means to pay regularly to get something, such as a magazine, a streaming service or a newspaper.
fact: «Subscribe» can also mean to sign up to get updates, emails or videos from a channel or website, often for free.
fact: In formal writing, «subscribe to» an idea means to agree with or support it.
fact: To work out the cost of a subscription that is paid each month, use maths: monthly cost × number of months.
exercise: «Mum will subscribe to the magazine so it arrives every month.» What does subscribe mean here? | To draw a picture of the magazine | To read the magazine in the library | To lose the magazine in the post | *To pay regularly to get the magazine
exercise: «Please subscribe to my channel for more videos.» What does subscribe mean here? | *To sign up to get new videos, often for free | To lend money to the channel's owner | To switch off the screen for ever | To pay the channel a fine
exercise: A magazine costs £4 a month. How much is it if you subscribe for 6 months? | £10 | £64 | *£24 | £46
test: «I don't subscribe to that view.» What does subscribe to mean here? | *To agree with or support the idea | To forget the idea | To buy a copy of the view | To be scared of the idea
test: Which phrase shows someone paying regularly for a service? | *Pay to subscribe to the streaming service each month | Ask a question about the video | Like and share the video | Look at the screen
exam: Mia says «subscribe» always means to pay money. Is she right? | No, it only means to read | Yes, it only means to pay for food | Yes, it always means to pay money | *No, it can also mean signing up for free updates or agreeing with an idea
